// Problem: Remove Outermost Parentheses
// LeetCode: 1021

// Approach:
// Use depth counter to identify outermost parentheses.
// Skip the first '(' and last ')' of every primitive string.

class Solution {
public:
    string removeOuterParentheses(string s) {
        string ans = "";
        int n = s.length();
        int p = 1;
        int i = 1;

        while(i < n){
            if(p == 0){
                continue;
            }
            else if(s[i] == '('){
                i++;
                p++;
                ans.push_back('(');
            }
            else if(s[i] == ')'){
                i++;
                p--;
                if(p > 0){
                    ans.push_back(')');
                }
            }
        }

        return ans;
    }
};

/*
TIME COMPLEXITY: O(n)
SPACE COMPLEXITY: O(n)

Mistakes:
1. Initially tried to remove parentheses directly using erase(), but modifying the same string
   changes indices and makes traversal difficult.
2. Used vector<int> for storing substrings, but substr() returns string.
3. Variable name "new" cannot be used because it is a C++ keyword.
4. Did not handle depth properly in the beginning, which made primitive identification complex.

Learning:
1. Depth counter can identify primitive parentheses boundaries.
2. Outermost brackets are the ones where depth becomes 1 and returns to 0.
3. Building a new answer string is safer than modifying the input string.
4. Learned erase() syntax in C++:
   string.erase(position, count)
   It is used to remove characters from a string.
5. Learned substr() syntax in C++:
   string.substr(starting_index, length)
   It is used to extract a part of a string.
6. Learned that modifying a string using erase() can shift indexes, so careful handling is required.
7. Understood how balance/depth can simplify nested parentheses problems.

Possible Optimization:
Use depth = 0 and traverse from the first character to avoid special handling of the first '('.
*/
